Title
-----

     ivfsw -- Conditional F-statistics of Sanderson and Windmeijer (2016)

Syntax
------

        ivfsw

    ivfsw is a postestimation command for use after ivregress or ivreg2.

Description
-----------

    ivfsw calculates the conditional F-statistic of Sanderson and Windmeijer
    (2016) for each endogenous variable (exposure) in the model most recently
    fitted by ivregress or ivreg2.  The conditional F-statistic for an
    exposure tests whether it is weakly identified given the other exposures,
    and is used to assess instrument strength in models with multiple
    exposures, such as in multivariable Mendelian randomization.

    For each exposure, ivfsw fits the conditional model of the exposure
    regressed on the other exposures and the exogenous variables, with the
    other exposures instrumented by the excluded instruments, and then
    computes the F-statistic of the excluded instruments in the regression of
    the residuals of the conditional model on the excluded instruments and
    exogenous variables. The conditional F-statistic is this F-statistic
    multiplied by L/(L - K + 1), where L is the number of excluded
    instruments and K the number of exposures, and has L - K + 1 numerator
    degrees of freedom. With a single exposure it is the usual first stage
    F-statistic for the excluded instruments.

    The conditional F-statistics can be compared with the Stock and Yogo
    (2005) critical values for a single exposure with L - K + 1 instruments.
    They are the same as the conditional F-statistics calculated by the fsw()
    function in the R package OneSampleMR, and as the Sanderson-Windmeijer
    F-statistics reported by ivreg2 with the first option.

    ivfsw uses the weights and the variance type of the fitted model. After a
    model fitted with robust or one-way cluster-robust standard errors
    (including with pweights) the F-statistics are robust versions, and after
    a model with weights the conditional models and F-tests are weighted, in
    each case matching ivreg2. With cluster-robust standard errors the
    denominator degrees of freedom are the number of clusters minus one. Note
    that the Stock and Yogo (2005) critical values were derived for the
    non-robust F-statistic. HAC, two-way cluster-robust, bootstrap, and
    jackknife variances, and the partial(), dofminus(), and sdofminus()
    options of ivreg2, are not supported.

Examples
--------

    Read in binary outcome data; y outcome, x1 and x2 exposures, w covariate,
    z* instrumental variables (genotypes).

        . use
            https://raw.githubusercontent.com/remlapmot/ivonesamplemr/main/da
            > ta/ivbinoutdata, clear

    Conditional F-statistics after ivregress.

        . ivregress 2sls y w (x1 x2 = z1 z2 z3)
        . ivfsw

    Conditional F-statistics after ivreg2.

        . ivreg2 y w (x1 x2 = z1 z2 z3)
        . ivfsw

    Robust conditional F-statistics.

        . ivregress 2sls y w (x1 x2 = z1 z2 z3), vce(robust)
        . ivfsw

Stored results
--------------

    ivfsw stores the following in r():

    Scalars        
      r(N)                number of observations
      r(K)                number of exposures (endogenous variables)
      r(L)                number of excluded instruments

    Macros         
      r(vce)              variance type used: unadjusted, robust, or
                            cluster-robust (clustvar)

    Matrices       
      r(table)            conditional F-statistics, numerator and denominator
                            degrees of freedom, and p-values, with one row
                            for each exposure

References
----------

    Sanderson E, Windmeijer F.  A weak instrument F-test in linear IV models
        with multiple endogenous variables.  Journal of Econometrics, 2016,
        190, 2, 212-221.  DOI

    Stock JH, Yogo M.  Testing for weak instruments in linear IV regression.
        In Andrews DWK, Stock JH (eds.), Identification and Inference for
        Econometric Models: Essays in Honor of Thomas Rothenberg. 2005.
        Cambridge University Press, Cambridge. 80-108.

